Feature: The OpenFeature Operator
  The OpenFeature Operator serves feature flags as Kubernetes objects: a FeatureFlag holds a set of flags, a FeatureFlagSource says where a workload's flags come from, and the operator's admission webhook adds a flagd sidecar that serves them to a pod that asks for it by a label and an annotation. It runs in a namespace of its own that a cluster's overlay selects. It is the one application of the tree that changes other workloads' pods at admission, so what its webhooks may see, what happens while it is down and where the images it puts into other pods are pinned are held by tests: nothing it registers can refuse, change or delay a pod that did not ask, and no workload asks.

  Scenario: The OpenFeature Operator is selected by overlay entries and builds nothing when it is not
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects the operator with two entries, the namespace directory open-feature-operator and the application's ks.yaml
    When it is built with both entries, with only the namespace directory's and with neither, and then with both and each application the operator depends on left out in turn
    Then with both it applies the namespace open-feature-operator, its app-template-defaults ConfigMap, its default-deny NetworkPolicy and the Kustomization open-feature-operator, which waits and names cluster-ca, prometheus-operator-crds and cilium; with only the namespace directory's, the namespace, the ConfigMap and the NetworkPolicy and nothing of the operator; with neither, nothing of the namespace; with one of the three left out it is refused, naming open-feature-operator and the missing application; and the release renders no Namespace of its own

  Scenario: The operator's webhooks see only a pod that asks, skip the system namespaces and fail open
    Given the operator's application built as Flux applies it and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values and the release's post-renderers
    When the webhook configurations, the Service they call and the roles of the release are read
    Then the release registers exactly two webhook configurations with one webhook each, both calling the operator's own Service; the mutating webhook matches creations and updates of pods and nothing else, only pods that carry the label openfeature.dev/enabled with the value true, in no namespace of kube-system, flux-system and open-feature-operator, with nothing that opts a namespace back in; the validating webhook matches creations and updates of FeatureFlags and nothing else; both fail open and wait 10 seconds at most; of the rights Kubernetes' documentation names as ways to escalate privilege, no role of the release grants any but the creation of workloads: no verb on the webhook configurations' API group, nothing on Secrets, service account tokens, persistent volumes, the nodes' proxy, namespaces or certificate approval, no wildcard, bind, escalate or impersonate, and on roles and bindings only reading and updating the one binding the operator adds flag readers to; nor does any role exec or attach into a pod, write to a node or its status, or aggregate other roles; nor is any binding to a role the release does not render

  Scenario: The images the operator injects are pinned by tag and digest in its values
    Given the operator's values and its pinned chart rendered with them
    When the image variables of the rendered Deployment, the container's own image and the chart's default values are read
    Then the sidecar's, flagd's and flagd-proxy's images each reach the operator as the chart's default repository and a tag that is the chart's default release of the plain build with a sha256 digest, the tag the values file holds under a comment Renovate reads, the sidecar and flagd are one image, pulled only when absent, the operator's own image is pinned the same way at the version the chart's values default to, Renovate moves the chart and that image in one group, and merges nothing of the application by itself

  Scenario: The operator's certificates come from the cluster's own CA
    Given the operator's application built as Flux applies it and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values and the release's post-renderers
    When the Certificates, the issuers, the Secrets and the webhook configurations' annotations are read
    Then the release holds two Certificates, the webhook's serving certificate and the metrics certificate, each naming the ClusterIssuer cluster-ca, no Issuer, ClusterIssuer or Secret, and no name of an ACME issuer; both webhook configurations ask the CA injector for the bundle of the serving certificate, whose names include the one the API server calls the webhook's Service by and whose Secret the pod mounts where the webhook server reads it

  Scenario: The operator's pod meets the restricted level with a read-only root and only its certificate mounts
    Given the operator's application as the lab-sto2 overlay applies it, its namespace and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values and the release's post-renderers
    When the namespace's labels, the exceptions list and the rendered Deployment are read
    Then the namespace enforces the restricted level with no entry in the exceptions list, the pod passes that level, running as a non-root user with the runtime's seccomp profile, its one container has a read-only root filesystem, no capability and no privilege escalation, the pod's volumes are the two certificate Secrets and nothing else, each mounted read-only, the Deployment has one replica, written as an integer, and the container's requests and limits are the chart's own, written out in the values

  Scenario: The operator's namespace denies traffic by default and its policies name only the webhook's callers, the kubelet, Prometheus and the API server
    Given the operator's application as the lab-sto2 overlay applies it, its namespace and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values and the release's post-renderers
    When the namespace's NetworkPolicy, the application's CiliumNetworkPolicies, its PodMonitor and the flows between the operator's pod and its peers are evaluated
    Then the namespace holds the default deny and is not listed, the policies select the operator's pod by the labels of the Deployment's selector and leave the deny to the namespace, the pod takes the kubelet's probes on its health port, Prometheus on the metrics port its PodMonitor scrapes and, by a policy of its own with its reason, the API server, the local host and other nodes' hosts on the webhook's port, it reaches the API server and nothing else, not the resolver, the world, the secret store or another application, the world, another application and Prometheus do not reach the webhook's port nor the API server the metrics port, and the manager serves its metrics in plain HTTP on that port while the chart's metrics Service names a port no container has

  Scenario: The operator's definitions stay when its release leaves, and what is written at run time is not undone as drift
    Given the operator's application built as the cluster's root builds it and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values and the release's post-renderers
    When the definitions the release renders, the HelmRelease's drift detection and the objects the operator and the CA injector write at run time are read
    Then the release renders the four definitions of core.openfeature.dev as templates, each kept when the release is uninstalled, with no entry for the group in the lists of kinds without a schema or of operators' definitions; drift detection is on and ignores the subjects of the ClusterRoleBinding the operator adds service accounts to, which the release renders with its own two and the operator's role may update; and the webhook configurations render no CA bundle, so the one the CA injector writes is no drift

  Scenario: No workload opts in, and the worked example builds without being applied
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay with every application it selects built as Flux applies it, the operator's render, and the example application hello-flags built from its own ks.yaml
    When the objects of the operator's API group, the pod templates' labels and annotations, the overlay's entries and the example's objects are read
    Then nothing the overlay builds and nothing the operator's chart renders is an object of core.openfeature.dev or carries a label or annotation of openfeature.dev, the overlay lists the example commented out with its reason and builds none of it, and the example holds one FeatureFlag with one flag, one FeatureFlagSource whose one source is that flag set by the kubernetes provider, a ServiceAccount of its own and a Deployment whose pod template carries the label and both annotations, names that source and that account, passes the restricted level before injection and asks the sidecar for that flag over the pod's loopback on the port the definition defaults to, with a policy that lets the kubelet reach the sidecar's management port and the pod reach the API server and nothing else; its kinds and versions are ones the pinned chart's definitions serve, it waits for open-feature-operator and cilium, and the catalogue has no entity for it
